    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d450">

                <pb facs="0516" n="504" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg504"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.2221/346: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Marseille</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Marseille</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-04-12T16:00:00+01:00">April 12, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:56 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of April 10, 1 p.m.<anchor n="59" xml:id="d450-f1"/> and
                    Department’s telegram No. 162, April 7, 7 p.m. to Paris.</p>
                <p>Judge at Coret believes that 15 Americans to be released April 16th will require
                    [apparent omission] to enter Spain. At trial all confessed they had attempted to
                    enter Spain to join army.</p>
                <p>Minister of Justice might authorize delivery to the Consulate of passports held
                    by tribunal should the Embassy so request.</p>
                <p>Does the Department desire representative of Consulate to attend trial of six
                    Americans at Perpignan April 14 and trial one American April 16? Less evidence
                    against them than in previous case.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
